Jeremiah DeWitt Weiner wrote:
>As far as non-pet monsters,
> it's a little silly that you can't tell what a monster is
> carrying/wearing unless you or it does something involving that item,
> but it might be a little too much of a change to the game dynamics if
> you could see that that dwarf was carrying a wand of death before it got
> within range.

It's not just a *little* silly -- it's downright stupid. That's one of
the few peeves I have about Nethack. There's no reason why doing the
"look" action at a given monster shouldn't tell you, at least, that
it's wearing a hooded cloak, a suit of dwarvish mithril, and an iron
helm, wielding a pick-axe, and has a ruby ring on its left hand (for
example.) This is one of those things in Nethack that simply makes no
sense at all, and I think should definitely be changed ASAP. (Of
course, monsters should then be able to see what armour/weapons you're
using, and (the intelligent ones at least) react accordingly.)

Now, as for what objects are in a monster's inventory, *that* should
remain secret. After all, the adventurer doesn't go around carrying all
his/her wands, tools, potions, etc. out in plain sight - undoubtedly
they're attached to a belt, stored in a waist pouch, or in an
easily-accessible "pack". There's no reason why the monster shouldn't
do the same. (Plus, if you *were* able to see a monster's entire
inventory by just looking at it, death drops would make no sense. I
suppose you can do this with a wand of probing, but who goes around
probing all the monsters in the dungeon?)

> It would also be nice if a (intelligent, speaking) pet
> could tell you things like "My hands are stuck to this spear!"

The need for this is unlikely to arise, as (IIRC) pets hardly ever even
step on something cursed -- and then they "move only reluctantly." They
certainly don't pick up cursed objects, and *definitely* don't wield
them. (It's only enemy monsters that do that. As to why becoming a pet
should suddenly give a monster the property of being able to tell apart
cursed vs. uncursed objects -- no clue. NHI definitely NRL.)

